      "id": 1,
      "text": "New Town was built between 1767 and 1850, preserving much of its original neo‑classical and Georgian architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Town%2C_Edinburgh"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Princes Street faces Edinburgh Castle across the former Nor Loch depression that separates the New Town from the Old Town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Town%2C_Edinburgh"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Assembly Rooms on George Street, the Balmoral Hotel’s clock tower above Waverley Station, and the Scott Monument are key New Town landmarks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Town%2C_Edinburgh"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 19th‑century Edinburgh, Leith and Newhaven Railway tunneled under New Town, linking Scotland Street with Canal Street near Waverley Station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Town%2C_Edinburgh"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "New Town Gardens received a heritage designation in 2001, protecting its historic layout and green space.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Town%2C_Edinburgh"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1995 the combined New Town, Old Town and West End were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
